## Step 1: Update Database Schema

Add `token_id` column to `CoffeeBatch` table to link PostgreSQL records with on-chain tokens.

**Migration**: Already applied in `database/models.py` (line 199)

```python
# database/models.py
class CoffeeBatch(Base):
    __tablename__ = 'coffee_batches'
    
    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True, index=True)
    batch_id = Column(String(100), unique=True, index=True)
    
    # ... existing fields ...
    
    # Blockchain token tracking (v1.6 - Dec 2025)
    token_id = Column(BigInteger, nullable=True, index=True)  # ERC-1155 token ID on CoffeeBatchToken contract
    
    # User ownership tracking
    created_by_user_id = Column(Integer, ForeignKey("user_identities.id"))
    created_by_did = Column(String(200), index=True)
    
    # Verification fields
    status = Column(String(30), default='PENDING_VERIFICATION', index=True)
    verified_quantity = Column(Float)  # Actual quantity verified (may differ from claimed)
    verification_timestamp = Column(DateTime)
    verifier_did = Column(String(200))
```

**Why `BigInteger`?**
- Solidity `uint256` can be very large (2^256 - 1)
- Python `int` has unlimited precision, but database needs explicit type
- `BigInteger` in SQLAlchemy maps to PostgreSQL `BIGINT` (up to 2^63 - 1)
- More than sufficient for sequential token IDs (1, 2, 3, ...)

**Verification**:
```bash
# Check if column exists
psql $DATABASE_URL -c "\\d coffee_batches" | grep token_id

# Should show:
# token_id | bigint | | nullable, indexed
```

